@@ -203,9 +203,9 @@ pstore records pile up in firmware (EFI) storage on systems without systemd-psto
 ### Curve stability
 Every CPU / GPU curve applied (tabs, scenes, game hooks, boot autoload) is journaled by the helpers; this page lays the saved Xid / GSP / D3cold and machine-check / lockup errors over it: hours in effect, errors, errors per 10 h per profile, highlighted when clearly worse than stock. Optional guard: *Take the GPU curve back to stock after an Xid* and the same for the CPU curve after a machine check — otherwise you only get a notification naming the curve. After such a reset the notification has *Undo*, which applies the saved profile that was in effect again (not for unsaved edits). The curve tabs also have **Trial apply**: after Apply you have 30 s to press *Keep*, else the curve goes back to stock.
 
-**Memory test** checks BIOS memory timings or speed: it writes and reads back a share of the free memory (10–90 %, default 70 %) for 5, 15, 30 or 60 minutes with `stressapptest` (preferred) or `memtester`, as you, no password, while machine checks reported by the Monitor count as errors. The machine is slow meanwhile. Each result is kept (newest 50, `~/.local/state/centurion/memtest.json`) with the BIOS memory timing settings it ran under — speed and the overridden timings, "changed this boot" when a new setting is not in effect yet; read without a password on the Legion Pro 7 16AFR10H, "not readable" elsewhere. memtester must lock the memory it tests: it is held to the session's memlock limit (`ulimit -l`), and a limit under 64 MiB is reported with how to raise it; stressapptest needs no lock. A failure also raises a *Curve guard* notification. Without either tool the section shows the install command.
+**Memory test** checks BIOS memory timings or speed: it writes and reads back a share of the free memory (10–90 %, default 70 %) for 5, 15, 30 or 60 minutes with `stressapptest` (preferred) or `memtester`, as you, no password, while machine checks reported by the Monitor count as errors. The machine is slow meanwhile. Each result is kept (newest 50, `~/.local/state/centurion/memtest.json`) with the BIOS memory timing settings it ran under — speed and the overridden timings, "changed this boot" when a new setting is not in effect yet; read without a password on the Legion Pro 7 16AFR10H, "not readable" elsewhere. memtester must lock the memory it tests: it is held to the session's memlock limit (`ulimit -l`), and a limit under 64 MiB is reported with how to raise it; stressapptest needs no lock. A failure also raises a *Curve guard* notification. If the kernel log needs a password on this machine, machine checks are not counted during the test, the section says so and each result is marked; only the tool's own result counts. Without either tool the section shows the install command.
 
-**GPU test** checks a graphics-card undervolt, overclock or VRAM offset: it runs work whose correct result is known on the chosen GPU (never an unnamed "first" one, so laptops pick the discrete card) for 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60 minutes (default 10) and counts every wrong result, as you, no password. Run **VRAM** after changing a memory offset (it fills 10–95 %, default 80 %, of the video memory) and **Core** after changing a core curve. The screen may stutter meanwhile. Needs the Vulkan driver of your GPU. A lost GPU, any wrong result, or an Xid / amdgpu error reported by the Monitor during the run is a failure. A pass is evidence, not proof. Each result is kept (newest 50, `~/.local/state/centurion/gpustress.json`) with the curve state in effect.
+**GPU test** checks a graphics-card undervolt, overclock or VRAM offset: it runs work whose correct result is known on the chosen GPU (never an unnamed "first" one, so laptops pick the discrete card) for 5, 10, 15, 30 or 60 minutes (default 10) and counts every wrong result, as you, no password. Run **VRAM** after changing a memory offset (it fills 10–95 %, default 80 %, of the video memory) and **Core** after changing a core curve. The screen may stutter meanwhile. Needs the Vulkan driver of your GPU. A lost GPU, any wrong result, or an Xid / amdgpu error reported by the Monitor during the run is a failure. A pass is evidence, not proof. Each result is kept (newest 50, `~/.local/state/centurion/gpustress.json`) with the curve state in effect. If the kernel log needs a password on this machine, Xid / amdgpu errors are not counted during the test, the section says so and each result is marked; only the tool's own result counts.
 
 ### Sleep
 Suspend mode, suspend statistics (failures, last failing device), hardware-sleep time, and one row per sleep from the sleep hook: duration, battery used and %/h, hardware-sleep share, NVIDIA dGPU state before → after, what woke the machine. **Wake sources**: untick an ACPI device to stop it waking the laptop (remembered; restored at boot by `centurion-tune`). **Suggestions**: an enabled wake source that woke the machine at least twice with the lid closed (neither the lid nor the power button among the wake sources) is listed with the battery the machine then spent awake before sleeping again, and a *Disable* button that does the same as unticking it. The battery cost is measured only on battery and when the next sleep came within 2 h.
